Consider a relativistic fluid description of the matter produced in high-energy nuclear collisions. Standard relativistic hydrodynamics is a zero-mean-free-path (zero correlation length) effective theory valid when the microscopic scale of the constituents (mean free path or correlation length) λ_micro is much smaller than the macroscopic hydrodynamical inhomogeneity scale λ_Hydro of the flow, i.e. when the dimensionless expansion parameter

   x = λ_micro / λ_Hydro

satisfies x ≪ 1. In realistic nuclear collisions, however, x is not negligible, and locally the inhomogeneity scale of the energy density, temperature, and flow fields may be comparable to λ_micro, so that the standard hydrodynamic gradient expansion ceases to be reliable—precisely in the regions and at the times most sensitive to the QCD equation of state and the phase transition.

Problem. Construct a systematic extension of relativistic hydrodynamics applicable to finite x = λ_micro/λ_Hydro that:
 (i) provides a controlled expansion (or resummation) in x reducing to ideal/dissipative hydrodynamics as x → 0;
 (ii) yields finite-size corrections to the local energy density, temperature, and flow profile in a manifestly covariant way;
 (iii) consistently treats the regime where λ_micro ∼ λ_Hydro, including the breakdown of local equilibrium near a phase transition;
 (iv) allows quantitative predictions for how finite-size corrections modify the inferred properties of the QCD phase transition (e.g. critical temperature, latent heat, order of the transition) extracted from observed collective behavior.

State and justify such an extended hydrodynamic framework, and determine the leading corrections to the local energy density as a function of x.

